<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Maroneia</h1><p class="st-meta">650 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Maroneia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chian colony on the coast of Thrace, famed since Homer for its strong wine.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Maroneia was founded by settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chios">Chios</a> c. 650 BCE on the Thracian coast east of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abdera">Abdera</a>, near the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicones">Cicones</a>. Its name recalled Maron, the priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> who gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> the potent wine he used to intoxicate the Cyclops.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Maroneia's wealth came from wine and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, and its coins showed a prancing horse and vines. It paid tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> and later joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-athenian-league">Second Athenian League</a>. The city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> sanctuary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> reflected its wine economy, and it built a strong harbour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Maroneia passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a>, then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V</a>'s troops massacred many citizens in 184 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> made it a free city, and it came under Roman control by 146 BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 650 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.77%;width:10.03%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>650 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maroneia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Dionysus</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maronia</dd></dl></aside></div>

